<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giusto de' Menabuoi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1320–c. 1390</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giusto de' Menabuoi was a Florentine painter who worked mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a> lords. His masterpiece is the fresco cycle of the Padua Baptistery, painted in the 1370s for Fina Buzzacarini, wife of Francesco I da Carrara, with a dome showing Christ surrounded by ranks of saints. His style followed the example of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giusto-de-menabuoi.jpg" alt="Giusto de' Menabuoi" width="320" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giusto de' Menabuoi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giusto_de%27_menabuoi,_paradiso_(detail),_1376-78,_battistero_di_Padova.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
